      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Overlapping implantation of bioresorbable scaffolds (BRSs) are frequent in long coronary lesions. Its impact on clinical outcomes is unknown."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To compare the clinical outcomes of patients treated with overlapping BRS with those patients treated with no-overlap BRS."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We analyzed the 1-year clinical outcomes of 1,477 patients treated with BRS in the GHOST-EU registry, according to the implantation of overlapping BRS. Primary endpoint was patient oriented composite endpoint (PoCE) of: all-cause death, any myocardial infarction (MI) and any repeated revascularization. Scaffold thrombosis, according to Academic Research Consortium definition, was also analyzed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "A total of 320 (21.7%) patients were treated with overlapping BRS (overlap group), whereas the remaining 1,157 (78.3%) received no-overlap BRS (no-overlap group). The overlap group had significantly higher frequency of male sex, diabetes mellitus, stable angina, B2/C lesion type, SYNTAX score ≥22, lesion length >34 mm, use of intracoronary imaging guidance, pre- and postdilatation. At 1-year, there were no differences in PoCE between the overlap versus no-overlap group (18.4% vs. 18.2%; HR 1.07, [0.80-1.44]; P\u2009=\u20090.636), even after adjustment (HR 1.05, [0.48-2.20]; P\u2009=\u20090.904). Scaffold thrombosis rate did not differ either at one-month (1.3% vs. 1.5%, P\u2009=\u20090.769) or at 1-year (1.9% vs. 2.1%, P\u2009=\u20090.823)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "In \"Real-world\" clinical practice, overlapping BRS does not appear to have an impact on clinical outcomes as compared to no-overlapping BRS. These preliminary data should be confirmed. © 2016 Wiley Periodicals, Inc."
          }
        ]
      },
